---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Quire — Lossless Office document engines in Rust"
  description="Quire builds lossless Office document engines in Rust. DOCX in private preview: byte-for-byte round-trip, Knuth-Plass line breaking, one core for native and browser."
  path="/"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="tick">●</span> Private preview — opens when the foundations hold</p>
      <h1>Lossless Office document engines in Rust.</h1>
      <p class="sub">
        Parts Quire doesn't touch are written back byte-for-byte. The layout pipeline —
        Knuth-Plass optimal line breaking, UAX#14 break rules, incremental pagination, real
        font metrics — produces display lists that both a native and a browser renderer
        consume from the same core.
      </p>
      <p class="sub muted" lang="zh-Hant" style="margin-top:0.9rem;">
        以 Rust 打造的無損 Office 文件引擎：未觸碰的部分原字節寫回，同一核心同時餵給原生與瀏覽器渲染器。
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/playground">Try the playground</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/roadmap">Read the roadmap</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" aria-labelledby="evidence-h">
    <h2 id="evidence-h" style="margin-top:0;">Evidence, not promises</h2>
    <div class="stats">
      <div>
        <span class="num">399/399</span>
        <span class="lbl">real-world corpus files round-trip byte-identical in CI — ZIP64 and unknown extensions included</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">2 × 2</span>
        <span class="lbl">CI matrix: ubuntu / Windows × native + wasm32-unknown-unknown</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">&lt; 54 KB</span>
        <span class="lbl">gzipped first wasm payload — CRDT collaboration code lives in a deferred chunk</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" aria-labelledby="formats-h">
    <h2 id="formats-h" style="margin-top:1rem;">One lossless core, three document formats</h2>
    <p>
      The bet: DOCX, XLSX and PPTX share most of what is hard — the OPC container
      (zip + XML, lossless at byte level), OOXML part/model handling, and the same
      lossless discipline. Each format reuses that core instead of forking it, and keeps
      the layout model its format demands.
    </p>
    <div class="formats">
      <article>
        <div class="fmt-tag"><span>DOCX</span><span class="fmt-state now">Now</span></div>
        <h3>Flow</h3>
        <p>Lossless round-trip plus the line-breaking and pagination pipeline — what the private preview runs on.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <div class="fmt-tag"><span>XLSX</span><span class="fmt-state">Next</span></div>
        <h3>Grid</h3>
        <p>Sibling format crate on the same OPC/lossless core: workbook model, formula engine, sheet render list.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <div class="fmt-tag"><span>PPTX</span><span class="fmt-state">Then</span></div>
        <h3>Canvas</h3>
        <p>Slide and shape model reusing the layout and shaping crates; its own presentation render list.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="muted" style="font-size:0.95rem;">
      Shared: OPC container, OOXML part machinery, lossless discipline. Not shared: the
      layout model — each format typesets the way its format says it should.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" aria-labelledby="principles-h">
    <h2 id="principles-h">Principles the build enforces</h2>
    <div class="rows">
      <div class="row">
        <div class="k">Lossless by construction</div>
        <p>Untouched ZIP entries are copied byte-for-byte on write-back; <code>roundtrip --check</code> plus a 400-file corpus gate it on every commit across two OSes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <div class="k">One core, two targets</div>
        <p>The engine compiles to native and <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code>. The browser shell is a thin platform adapter, not a second implementation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <div class="k">Layering, machine-checked</div>
        <p>Format crates never import shells; shells enter through one engine API. A CI script enforces the boundary instead of a code review convention.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <div class="k">Real typesetting</div>
        <p>Shaped advances against real font metrics, Knuth-Plass optimal breaks, incremental pagination with p95 reflow under 8 ms.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" aria-labelledby="waitlist-h">
    <h2 id="waitlist-h">Opening soon</h2>
    <p>
      The engine is in private preview while the foundations — XLSX groundwork and the
      collaborative layer — hold. When the site opens, this section becomes a one-field
      waitlist; until then, write to
      <a href="mailto:preview@quireoffice.dev">preview@quireoffice.dev</a> if you want
      early access to the playground build.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
